 "78255a1e4c1f9166f628f62233729cee80ed3ddfc5d9d5d5b049e67fa2ffa056" "basis" 2629 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072286/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-338004" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "homogeneous"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072286/state.json" "file_sha256" "78255a1e4c1f9166f628f62233729cee80ed3ddfc5d9d5d5b049e67fa2ffa056" "basis" 2629 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072286/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-338004" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "inhomogeneous"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072286/state.json" "file_sha256" "78255a1e4c1f9166f628f62233729cee80ed3ddfc5d9d5d5b049e67fa2ffa056" "basis" 2629 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072286/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-338004" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "inhomogeneous"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072286/state.json" "file_sha256" "78255a1e4c1f9166f628f62233729cee80ed3ddfc5d9d5d5b049e67fa2ffa056" "basis" 2629 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072286/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-339254" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\n- $x{y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+4y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2x,\\,y(-1)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=0$\n- ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}+y=8$\n- $x{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-3y={e}^{x},\\,y(1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=4$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072286/state.json" "file_sha256" "78255a1e4c1f9166f628f62233729cee80ed3ddfc5d9d5d5b049e67fa2ffa056" "basis" 2629 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072286/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-339254" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y,$ then this is an **initial value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is not an initial value problem.  It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=-1$ and $x=2$). This is a **boundary value problem.**\n\n- The second system is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified.\n\n- The third system is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$)." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072286/state.json" "file_sha256" "78255a1e4c1f9166f628f62233729cee80ed3ddfc5d9d5d5b049e67fa2ffa056" "basis" 2629 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072286/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-339254" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072286/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "56ce1d7afacf04f