veloper/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14074190/state.json" "file_sha256" "9e86ec5c87e6cb4ebe25403fa76a012ddaca79b0a6a8fd49950f6b37e798e127"} {"question" "q-337990" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine which of the following second-order ODEs are homogeneous, and which are inhomogeneous.$\\,$ The equation $\\,{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+3x{y}^{′}-y=0$ is {{field-1}}. \n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}+y={e}^{x}$ is {{field-2}}. \n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,{y}^{″}+{x}^{2}y+x=0$ is {{field-3}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14074190/state.json" "file_sha256" "9e86ec5c87e6cb4ebe25403fa76a012ddaca79b0a6a8fd49950f6b37e798e127"} {"question" "q-337990" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose we have a second-order linear ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe equation is homogeneous if $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$ If it is not homogeneous, then it is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each option.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+3x{y}^{′}-y=0\n$$\n\nis $\\text{homogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}+y={e}^{x}\n$$\n\nis $\\text{inhomogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)={e}^{x},$ which is different from zero.\n\n- Writing the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{x}^{2}y+x=0\n$$\n\nin the form $a{y}^{″}+b{y}^{′}+cy=f,$ we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{x}^{2}y=-x.\n$$\n\nThis equation is $\\text{inhomogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=-x,$ which is different from zero." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14074190/state.json" "file_sha256" "9e86ec5c87e6cb4ebe25403fa76a012ddaca79b0a6a8fd49950f6b37e798e127"} {"question" "q-337990" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "cate